WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.460 --> 00:00:02.560
When in doubt, hit 'em with a twist.

00:00:02.820 --> 00:00:06.520
That seems to have been the thought process
of Avengers: Endgame screenwriters Christopher

00:00:06.520 --> 00:00:10.940
Markus and Stephen McFeely, who recently sat
down with the New York Times to discuss the

00:00:10.940 --> 00:00:13.940
film's plot in the wake of its runaway success.

00:00:14.170 --> 00:00:18.080
The twist in question occurs early in the
film, when the remaining Avengers track down

00:00:18.090 --> 00:00:22.779
Thanos, now living on the lonely planet he
retreated to after snuffing out half of all

00:00:22.780 --> 00:00:24.160
life in the universe.

00:00:24.340 --> 00:00:29.300
Their goal: to swipe the Infinity Stones and
use them to undo the Mad Titan's damage.

00:00:29.720 --> 00:00:33.800
But upon arriving, they find that Thanos has
used the power of the stones to reduce them

00:00:33.800 --> 00:00:36.200
to atoms, rendering their mission moot.

00:00:36.640 --> 00:00:40.290
In a moment of pure frustration, Thor does
what he should have done during the Battle

00:00:40.290 --> 00:00:44.730
of Wakanda and went for the head — killing
the most formidable foe the Avengers had ever

00:00:44.730 --> 00:00:47.320
faced roughly 20 minutes into the movie.

00:00:47.629 --> 00:00:52.080
It was a shocking narrative decision, one
which Markus and McFeely admitted they struggled

00:00:52.080 --> 00:00:52.940
to pin down.

00:00:53.220 --> 00:00:55.000
McFeely told the New York Times:

00:00:55.440 --> 00:00:57.000
"We always had this problem.

00:00:57.000 --> 00:00:58.749
[Thanos] has the ultimate weapon.

00:00:58.749 --> 00:01:00.180
He can see [an attack] coming.

00:01:00.380 --> 00:01:01.300
It's ridiculous.

00:01:01.600 --> 00:01:05.600
[...] At some point, [executive producer]
Trinh Tran went, 'Can't we just kill him?'

00:01:05.900 --> 00:01:08.240
And we all went, 'What happens if you just
kill him?

00:01:08.360 --> 00:01:09.440
Why would you kill him?

00:01:09.720 --> 00:01:11.070
Why would he let you kill him?'"

00:01:11.700 --> 00:01:16.100
The answer to that question is simple: Thanos
had accomplished what he set out to do.

00:01:16.320 --> 00:01:17.120
Markus explained:

00:01:17.560 --> 00:01:19.460
"It reinforced Thanos' agenda.

00:01:19.680 --> 00:01:20.340
He was done.

00:01:20.680 --> 00:01:25.280
Not to make him too Christ-like, but it was
like,'If I've got to die, I can die now.'"

00:01:26.080 --> 00:01:26.780
"And then what?

00:01:29.000 --> 00:01:30.040
I finally rest.

00:01:31.120 --> 00:01:33.880
And watch the sunrise on a grateful universe."

00:01:34.380 --> 00:01:37.960
Thor's brutal beheading of the Mad Titan benefited
no one, however.

00:01:37.960 --> 00:01:42.140
It was an act of pure frustration, and one
which was never going to bring back the Infinity

00:01:42.160 --> 00:01:44.859
Stones or any of the Avengers' fallen comrades.

00:01:45.500 --> 00:01:50.380
In fact, it seems likely that the noble Asgardian's
desperate, vengeful killing of Thanos may

00:01:50.380 --> 00:01:54.740
have been the final straw in causing him to
spiral into a years-long bout with depression.

00:01:55.350 --> 00:01:59.720
But this also gave Thor, who has gone from
a rather one-note hero to arguably the most

00:01:59.729 --> 00:02:05.180
fascinating and complex character in the MCU,
one of Endgame's most emotionally satisfying

00:02:05.180 --> 00:02:05.880
arcs.

00:02:06.400 --> 00:02:11.360
It wasn't until his encounter with his mother
Frigga in 2013, during the Avengers' time

00:02:11.360 --> 00:02:15.400
heist to recover the Infinity Stones, that
he came to terms with his failure.

00:02:15.830 --> 00:02:19.840
It all fell into place neatly for Markus and
McFeely, who had gone into the screenwriting

00:02:19.840 --> 00:02:24.480
process with little idea of where they could
take the Asgardian's personal journey during

00:02:24.480 --> 00:02:24.980
Endgame.

00:02:25.180 --> 00:02:26.180
Markus said:

00:02:26.660 --> 00:02:30.840
"When we were spitballing for Endgame, we
started with, Thor's on a mission of vengeance.

00:02:30.940 --> 00:02:34.300
And then we were like, he was on a mission
of vengeance in the last movie.

00:02:34.380 --> 00:02:36.740
This is all this guy ever does!

00:02:37.000 --> 00:02:39.420
[...] Let's drive him into a wall and see
what happens."

00:02:39.780 --> 00:02:44.640
In the end, by compounding his failure to
stop Thanos in a moment of weakness, the near-immortal

00:02:44.640 --> 00:02:48.300
god was made more relatable to the audience
than he had ever been before.

00:02:48.940 --> 00:02:52.740
Will Marvel continue exploring the journey
of Odinson in Phase 4 and beyond?

00:02:52.740 --> 00:02:56.600
Of all the original Avengers, it makes the
most sense for the God of Thunder's story

00:02:56.600 --> 00:03:01.060
to continue and Chris Hemsworth has proven
himself more than capable of taking the character

00:03:01.060 --> 00:03:04.120
to more interesting places than anyone ever
expected.

00:03:04.680 --> 00:03:09.230
But will he play a key role in Guardians of
the Galaxy Vol. 3, going into action to face

00:03:09.230 --> 00:03:10.700
some new cosmic threat?

00:03:11.060 --> 00:03:15.180
Will he return for another crossover team-up
film at some point in the MCU's future?

00:03:15.180 --> 00:03:18.420
Or will he become the first Avenger to get
a fourth solo flick?

00:03:18.740 --> 00:03:20.180
Only time will tell.

